#{[17592186404754 #edb/ref 1080 "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404754 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404754 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ffef5c19-fd74-5ba0-af4d-7b043c0b9bc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404755 #edb/ref 1080 "An ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404755 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404755 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f3838d79-b19d-50cb-bda4-8a032786b6e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404756 #edb/ref 1080 "A circle of radius $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404756 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404756 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c768ce44-3d75-5d9d-b7e6-297ae3bec501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404757 #edb/ref 1080 "A hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404757 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404757 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ddd23b0-eb68-5321-80ed-2e347cdf3572" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404758 #edb/ref 1080 "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404758 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404758 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bff4817a-3051-5961-a312-060e27c50a46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405181 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405181 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405181 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405181 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ find unit vectors along the principal axes of the quadratic form." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186405181 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "091c06c4-0cfd-5952-9df6-eba9b260f93a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405181 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the quadratic form \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A+4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, unit vectors along the principal axes of our quadratic form are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186405182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405182 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17