1075 #edb/ref 17592186551767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552389 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552389 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552389 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186552001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552389 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e8eb62ad-1414-5962-8117-83c1774feaed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552405 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552405 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552405 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552405 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve $7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-8{x}_{2}^{2}=9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552405 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f7fea28-dc13-51b3-b3d3-9108cf056cc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552405 #edb/ref 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-8{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=9\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=8\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-9)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A+9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552421 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552421 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97997" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552421 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552421 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=1.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ find the principal axes of the curve." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552421 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24473d8c-fe55-5d76-b96c-10c4724ef4f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552421 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=1\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552454 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552454 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95060" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552454 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552454 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve $14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}=15.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552454 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71a50d5a-430c-5d52-842d-aa1bb8a09b41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552454 #edb/ref 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=15\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=15\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-15I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-15[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-15I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552466 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140512" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552466 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552466 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6f/6f878dc844ba17668a2298c38a47b3af5684acedbae6e2c0934c396f9cbff0a5.png)\n\nWhich of the following diagrams depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552466 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "84285f76-9289-52c2-aa2b-3324df33ee37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552466 #edb/ref 1074 "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](images/aa/aaa23fd66546dbeb138c1dca851a2eb826a892edbde4cc7b5efafa29d6195580.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552487 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552487 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94998" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552487 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552487 #edb/ref 1071 "Which curve is defined by the equation ${x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=6?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552487 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a50d3b17-83c9-5e15-b240-68b23f1a29a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552487 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form. \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $2{y}_{1}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the given curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the two parallel lines ${y}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${y}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552513 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552513 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve $5{x}_{1}^{2}+24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}=52.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1eb45be-51da-5fe4-b582-bc025ec9533a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552513 #edb/ref 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=52\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=13\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-13.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=13,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-13I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{aligned}]-13[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-13I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-13,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-13)I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{aligned}]+13[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}18 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-13I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
